  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nAfternoon RAP analysis reveals flow aloft becoming slightly more \nzonal today across the Upper Great Lakes as the persistent western \nCONUS ridge is flattened out by a shortwave disturbance apparent in \nwater vapor imagery over southern Manitoba and producing plentiful \nlightning along the Canadian border. In the meantime, pleasant \nconditions remain in place across the UP ahead of this feature, with \nanother day of mostly sunny skies and temperatures in the upper 70s \nand low 80s as of mid Friday afternoon.\n\nThe upstream shortwave feature will track across northern Ontario \nand Lake Superior tonight, dragging a weak surface cold front across \nthe UP and bringing a round of showers and thunderstorms late \ntonight into Saturday morning. Still do not expect these storms to \nbe severe given the time of day and limited instability to work \nwith, but can't rule out a strong storm or two given 500-1000 J/kg \nMUCAPE and bulk shear values strengthening to around 35-40 kt \nthrough the morning. The better, albeit highly conditional, chance \nfor any strong to severe storms looks to come behind the front in \nthe afternoon as the air mass quickly recovers and allows MUCAPE \nvalues to build to 1500-2500 J/kg while effective bulk shear \ncontinues to increase to 40-45 kt. SPC has expanded it's marginal \nrisk for severe storms over the central and western UP for Saturday \nafternoon as several CAMs to try to develop a few storms over mainly \nthe southcentral UP for Saturday afternoon. Still, there are a few \nlimiting factors as the best forcing will have already departed to \nthe east and model soundings indicate at least some degree of \ncapping between 850-700 mb. Overall severe probabilities remain low \nas a result (5% for large hail or damaging wind gusts), but the \nenvironment will be favorable for any cells that can manage to \ndevelop Saturday afternoon. Rainfall amounts through Saturday still \ndo not look overly impressive as  probs for a quarter inch or more \ntop out at around 20% per the HREF and 40% per NBM. \n\nSunday and Monday offer additional chances for showers and \nthunderstorms as another pair of disturbances traverse the Great \nLakes, but the 12z guidance unfortunately does not offer much more \nclarity than previous cycles as models continue to struggle with \ntiming and placement of these features. Sunday could again feature a \nfavorable thermodynamic/kinematic environment as temperatures climb \nwell into the 80s to around 90 degrees near the WI border while \nsurface dewpoints rise to near 70 degrees, producing ample \ninstability while shear remains more than sufficient for storm \norganization. The limiting factor may once again prove to be forcing \nas the first impulse looks to be rather weak and questions remain \nregarding the track of this feature. SPC maintains a marginal risk \nfor severe weather over most of the UP on Sunday, with a small \nsliver of slight risk touching the far western UP. Monday's system \nlooks to be a bit stronger, but questions remain over timing and \nplacement and SPC continues to favor a more southern solution for \ntrack which would keep the larger severe threat confined to the \nsouth over parts of WI and Lower MI.\n\nLooking further into next week, ensembles continue to favor the \nreturn of a broad northwest flow pattern over the Great Lakes as the \ndownstream ridge reamplifies over the western and central CONUS. \nThis will favor temps close to seasonal norms through the middle of \nthe week, slowly increasing with gradual height rises towards the \nend of the week. The forecast looks mostly dry beyond Monday for \nnow, but can expect embedded disturbances to bring periodic chances \nfor at least some precipitation through the week.",
